WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.840
- [Narrator] This is a
story of boy meets girl

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.490
and the algorithm that
brought them true love.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.073
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.909 --> 00:00:11.409
(piano music)

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.530
The boy, Baraka, from Washington D.C.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.870
is gentle, laid back and attentive.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.570
The girl, Calaya, from Seattle

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.803
is assertive, creative and persistent.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.560
While their initial encounter
was love at first sight,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.860
this match was no accident.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.790
The union of Baraka and Calaya

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.633
was planned in exacting detail.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.720
- Baraka and Calaya are a perfect match.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.650
Our algorithm predicted it.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.560
The Gorilla Species
Survival Plan is responsible

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.490
for managing the demographic, genetic,

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:56.010
and overall population features

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.613
of the gorillas living
in North American Zoos.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.690
One could say we function
like a high-tech,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.230
heartfelt dating site for gorillas

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.593
but without all the swiping.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.420
The algorithm we use, was
created in the late '80s,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.190
which is several years before
it's human counterpart.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.300
We used data points on
genetics and relatedness

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.650
and combined that with what we know

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:22.450
about gorilla personality
and individual needs.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.990
We combine all that information

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.263
to make the best decisions for pairings.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.190
- [Narrator] Like any modern couple,

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.790
Baraka and Calaya
matched on a dating site,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.810
fell in love, and moved in together

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.483
with Calaya making the
cross country trip to D.C.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.830
After two years together,

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:42.653
Baraka and Calaya knew it
was time to start a family.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.850
- Western Lowland gorillas

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.670
are a critically endangered species.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.460
Our goal is to try to identify
the right male gorillas

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.830
and the right female gorillas
that will come together

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.560
to create baby gorillas,
so that our population

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.030
will carry on for a very long time.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.480
These creatures are extraordinary.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.820
Humans and gorillas share about
98 percent of the same DNA.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.240
We are similar in so many ways.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.440
Can gorillas experience
love the same way humans do?

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.113
We're not sure but why not
give them the best shot.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.440
- [Narrator] At 6:25
p.m., on April 15th, 2018,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.070
Baraka and Calaya's son Moke, was born.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.363
The algorithm worked.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.390
Moke will one day also be a part

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.520
of the Gorilla Species Survival Plan,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.943
waiting for his chance to find true love.

